[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63726,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "<p>Intravenous leiomyomatosis is an intravascular growth of benign smooth muscle cells in the absence of or beyond the confines of a leiomyoma, sometimes with pelvic or extrapelvic extension.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:15:15"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63727,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8890/1 Intravenous leiomyomatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63728,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E86.0 &amp; XH60C2 Leiomyoma of uterus &amp; Intravascular leiomyomatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63729,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> intravascular leiomyomatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63730,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63731,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intravenous leiomyomatosis is more commonly seen in the uterus; it rarely involves the broad ligament, pelvic veins, and vena cava {25675187}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63732,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients have symptoms similar to those encountered in patients with leiomyomas {21777942; 8112951; 3202247; 29699902}. Less commonly, they present with chest pain, dyspnoea, syncope, or pulmonary embolism due to right heart or pulmonary artery involvement {25675187; 11904348; 30898968}. Pelvic MRI may help to detect early-stage disease {28643137; 17179374}; CT-angiography and contrast-enhanced CT are useful if there is extension to extrapelvic vasculature {29395222; 26858203}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63733,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>Intravenous leiomyomatosis is uncommon {25675187; 29699902; 8112951; 3692675}. It occurs over a broad age range (median patient age: 45&nbsp;years).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:15:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63734,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63735,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The monoclonality of this benign smooth muscle proliferation is supported by X&nbsp;chromosome inactivation. t(12;14), involving <em>HMGA2</em>, and HMGA2 protein overexpression have been identified in a subset of cases, suggesting a pathogenetic relationship with uterine leiomyomas {11904348; 12508249; 26892441}. Recurrent 22q and 1p regional losses and 12q gains have also been reported, but no <em>MED12</em> mutations, unlike in leiomyomas {25033729}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63736,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multiple white, rubbery, and nodular myometrial masses are sometimes seen as worm-like tumour plugs in vessels, but this may be a subtle finding or not grossly visible {3202247}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63737,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>There is intravascular growth of benign smooth muscle cells, resembling typical leiomyoma or its subtypes, in the absence of or outside a leiomyoma {3202247; 25675187}; hydropic change, hyalinization, and thick-walled vessels are frequent {8112951; 3202247}. Rarely, endometrial stroma and glands may be seen admixed with the smooth muscle component (termed intravascular adenomyomatosis) {24076777}. Smooth muscle markers are generally positive and CD10 is negative.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:16:08"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63738,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63739,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-05T17:00:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63740,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> intravascular growth of benign smooth muscle tumour&nbsp;cells in the absence of or outside a leiomyoma.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:16:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63741,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This neoplasm is not usually staged.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-08-12T12:30:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63742,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 239,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "<p>Extrauterine extension occurs in about 30% of patients, involving pelvic veins, the inferior vena cava, and (rarely) heart or pulmonary vessels, leading to sudden death {3675155; 29351101}. Recurrence, the risk of which is about 10%, may occur years later, either within veins or rarely as benign metastasizing leiomyoma {25675187; 21777942; 3202247; 1203870}. For unresectable lesions, tamoxifen, aromatase inhibitors, or GnRH could be used {18280477}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T16:16:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]